# Stack & versions (do not re-litigate)

- Next.js 16 (App Router), TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS v4
- Supabase (Postgres + Auth) — single table `leads`, owner-only RLS
  (`auth.uid() = user_id`)
- Zod — one schema for the lead form (`leadSchema`)
- React Hook Form + `zodResolver`
- Zustand — UI-only state: active status filter, modal open/closed + mode
  (add/edit) + which lead is being edited
- TanStack Query — fetching `leads`, mutations (create/update/delete).
  **Optimistic update on inline status change is the main new concept
  this project is meant to teach.**
- UI components: shadcn/ui — use it for styling/primitives, don't
  hand-build what it already provides
- Icons: Lucide (via shadcn/ui's Nova preset) — no second icon library,
  same convention as Areej and the portfolio
- Dates: use `date-fns` for any date formatting/manipulation — don't
  hand-roll date logic

# When to use a library vs. build it yourself

- Core learning stack (Zod, Zustand, TanStack Query, RHF, Supabase/SQL,
  TypeScript patterns) — Amar builds this himself, no shortcuts.
- Anything outside that list where a small, well-known library fully
  solves the problem (styling primitives → shadcn/ui, dates → date-fns) —
  use the library, don't reinvent it.

# Folder structure & naming

Same conventions as the Areej project — reuse them, don't redefine from
scratch:

- Feature-based structure under `features/leads/` (`components/`,
  `hooks/`, `api/`, `schema.ts`, `store.ts`)
- Folders `kebab-case`, component files `PascalCase.tsx`, hooks
  `camelCase.ts` prefixed `use`
- Zustand store exported as `useLeadsUIStore`, Zod schema `leadSchema`
- Components never call `supabase.from(...)` directly — only through
  `use*` hooks calling pure `get*` / mutate helpers in
  `features/leads/api/`

# Brand color (reuse only — do not redesign)

Source: Amar's live portfolio palette. Paste as-is — no fifth hue, no
mint accent.

```css
:root {
  --forest: #0e2a26;
  --cream: #f3eee4;
  --mustard: #e4b52a;
  --sage: #9bb3a0;

  --background: var(--forest);
  --foreground: var(--cream);
  --primary: var(--mustard);
  --primary-foreground: var(--forest);
  --ring: var(--mustard);
}
```

- Dark surfaces: forest background, cream text, sage for quiet/muted
  labels, mustard as the one accent (CTA, hover, focus ring).
- Light surfaces (if any): cream background, forest text.
- Full ramps, contrast tables, and reuse notes: `my-color-system.md`
  (Amar's reference file) — this block above is the only part that
  actually needs to be wired into `globals.css`.
- Wiring this into `globals.css` + the Tailwind theme is a config-only
  task (see the code-writing exceptions in `mentor-behavior.mdc`) —
  Cursor can do this one directly.

# Git conventions

- Commit format: `type(scope): description` — e.g.
  `feat(leads): add status filter`, `fix(leads): prevent double submit on
create`.
- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `style`, `docs`, `chore`, `test`.
- Work directly on `main`, commit at each logical checkpoint inside a
  task (see the git workflow in `mentor-behavior.mdc`). No
  feature-branch-per-task by default at this project's size.
- After every finished task, Cursor tells Amar the exact commit
  command(s) and message(s) to use — not optional, see
  `mentor-behavior.mdc`.
